<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Fontainebleau (1762)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Fontainebleau (1762) was a secret agreement of 3 November 1762 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, to its ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Made as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> was ending, it compensated Spain for losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and relieved France of a colony it could no longer defend. It created Spanish Louisiana, which Spain returned to France in 1800.</p></div>
